WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.789
Welcome to Resgrid Academy.

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:05.739
In this lesson, we will record routine checks and missed work.

00:00:06.179 --> 00:00:10.130
Paper checks make it difficult to see which apparatus was inspected and

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.800
which scheduled checks were missed.

00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:15.289
We will explain the features, the setup decisions, and a

00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:17.229
practical way to check the result.

00:00:17.999 --> 00:00:22.390
A daily apparatus check is scheduled for each engine, and the weekly compliance

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.339
report shows any missed checks.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.940
Recorded checks show what was inspected and what was missed.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:32.009
The examples in this series use a fictional department and are teaching

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.289
scenarios, not default settings.

00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:36.100
Checklist Templates.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.759
Ready-made checklists for common checks.

00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:42.760
Start from a template or build a checklist for each apparatus.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.140
Checklists.

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:46.179
Checklists members complete.

00:00:46.709 --> 00:00:49.810
Create the checklists members complete on paper today.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.640
Checklist schedules and due work.

00:00:52.999 --> 00:00:55.070
Recurring schedules and due checks.

00:00:55.499 --> 00:00:58.349
Schedule each checklist and choose who completes it.

00:00:58.849 --> 00:01:01.429
Availability also depends on Checklists.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.009
Check access in the target department.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:05.550
New Checklist.

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:07.159
Create a checklist.

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:10.919
Pick the checks you do on paper today and who completes them.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.259
Checklist compliance reports.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.909
Completed, skipped and overdue checks over time.

00:01:17.269 --> 00:01:20.399
Pick the checks you do on paper today and who completes them.

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:23.300
Availability also depends on Checklists.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.850
Check access in the target department.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.180
For the training scenario, work in this order.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.239
Step 1.

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:34.160
Start from a checklist template or build the approved checks.

00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:35.329
Step 2.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.539
Assign the checklist and set its recurring schedule.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:39.730
Step 3.

00:01:40.169 --> 00:01:44.120
Complete a demo check, record a defect, and review compliance.

00:01:44.749 --> 00:01:46.290
Now verify the result.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:50.589
Compare due checks with completed, skipped, and overdue results.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.659
Confirm defects have an assigned follow-up owner.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.299
A common problem to watch for: A completion report

00:01:57.299 --> 00:01:59.009
documents recorded checks.

00:01:59.379 --> 00:02:03.490
A failed item still requires action through the department repair process.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:05.670
Your practice task is simple.

00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:10.220
Complete a daily Engine 1 check and leave a second demo check overdue.

00:02:10.689 --> 00:02:13.369
Confirm that the compliance view distinguishes both.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.770
Record the result, who owns the follow-up, and when it should be reviewed.

